    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="46.2">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Hn">Hn</ref> 46.2</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hn 46.2" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="570" ed="#liebermann"/>De quibuscumque<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="s" target="#Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.s"/> implacitetur aliquis, furst<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="t" target="#Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.t"/> et fandung<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="22" target="#Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.22"/> habeat; et nominentur ei placita, et inde ad VII<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="u" target="#Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.u"/> dies respondeat quod uelit.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 570 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.s" n="s" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">570</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl.</hi> 61, 20</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cf. 61, 20</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.t" n="t" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">570</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Frist und</hi> (= <hi rend="italic">zum</hi>) <hi rend="italic">Beweis</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">term and (= for) proof</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.u" n="u" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">570</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">s. o. Anm. n</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">see above note n</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.22" n="22" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">570</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">fon. <hi rend="italic">Sc Hg</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">fon. Sc Hg</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_46-2.lnote.oe.a" n="a" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">322</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Rechtsgang 14a ff.; Beweistermin 2. Agsächs. heisst <hi rend="italic">fandian</hi> ‘prüfen, untersuchen, erforschen’ (jetzt <hi rend="italic">to fand</hi>, unser ‘<hi rend="italic">fahnden</hi>’), <hi rend="italic">fandung</hi> ‘Beweis, Probe’, <hi rend="italic">fandere</hi> ‘temptor’ (Napier <hi rend="italic">Lex</hi>. 92), <hi rend="italic">fanding</hi> mengl. ‘Versuchung’. Die Phrase ist ἓν διὰ δυοῖν: ‘Beweisfrist’.—<hi rend="italic">Vgl</hi>. Opet <hi rend="italic">Processeinl</hi>. 6—<hi rend="italic">nominetur ei p., et i. a VII</hi>—46, 2a <hi rend="italic">Cum a</hi>. Rs</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Rechtsgang 14a ff.; Beweistermin 2. In Anglo-Saxon <hi rend="italic">fandian</hi> means ‘to test, examine, investigate’ (now <hi rend="italic">to fand</hi>, German ‘<hi rend="italic">fahnden</hi>’), <hi rend="italic">fandung</hi> ‘proof, trial’, <hi rend="italic">fandere</hi> ‘temptor’ (Napier <hi rend="italic">Lex</hi>. 92), and Middle English <hi rend="italic">fanding</hi> ‘temptation’. The phrase is ἓν διὰ δυοῖν: ‘term for proof’.—<hi rend="italic">Cf</hi>. Opet <hi rend="italic">Processeinl</hi>. 6—<hi rend="italic">nominetur ei p., et i. a VII</hi>—46, 2a <hi rend="italic">Cum a</hi>. Rs</seg></note>
      </div>
